# dbt validation commands

Reference for review rules and `verify-ddm-dbt`. Do not duplicate these blocks in other rules. Record what actually ran in [agent-output-contract](agent-output-contract.mdc) (`PASS` / `FAIL` / `NOT RUN` / `NOT AVAILABLE`).

`dbt parse` does not connect to the warehouse. `dbt compile`, `build`, `run`, `test`, and `source freshness` do.

## Local automated checks

No warehouse. Proof of project definition only.

```bash
dbt deps
dbt parse --no-partial-parse
```

When this repo has the verifier skill, run that instead of ad-hoc parse (it wraps deps/parse plus manifest and static Redshift checks):

```bash
.cursor/skills/verify-ddm-dbt/scripts/verify.sh changed
```

Do not treat skipped `compile`/`build` as a failed local environment.

## Redshift / Civis checks

Unavailable locally unless a warehouse connection exists (VDI + profile). Do not list these as passed unless they ran against Redshift.

```bash
dbt compile --select <model>
dbt build --select <model>+
```

Debug split:

```bash
dbt run --select <model>+
dbt test --select <model>+
```

`compile` can still hit the adapter for relation caching and introspective macros. It is not a warehouse-free syntax proof.

## Incremental models

Do not assume every column or logic change requires `--full-refresh`. Decide from config and whether history must change:

| Change | Expected action |
|--------|-----------------|
| New column, future rows only, compatible `on_schema_change` | Incremental run may be enough |
| New column requiring historical values | Full refresh or targeted backfill |
| Removed or renamed column | Check `on_schema_change`, consumers, and deployment behavior |
| Metric logic affecting historical rows | Full refresh or bounded backfill |
| Incremental predicate change | Test existing-table path, full-build path, and late-arriving data |
| `unique_key` change | High-risk review and duplicate/reconciliation tests |

```bash
dbt run --select <model>
dbt run --select <model> --full-refresh   # only when the table above says so
dbt run --select <model>                  # second incremental run — the path Civis uses daily
dbt test --select <model>+
```

State the refresh/backfill choice in the PR. After a full refresh, the second incremental run is required.

## PR / multi-model validation

Use state selection only when a baseline manifest from the comparison
environment is available:

```bash
dbt build \
  --select state:modified+ \
  --state <baseline-artifact-directory>
```

Do not use the current branch's `target/manifest.json` as its own baseline.

Baseline sources (repo overlay may name the real one): last successful production run artifacts, main-branch Civis artifacts, or a manifest saved before the branch started.

If no baseline manifest is available:

```bash
dbt build --select <changed_models>+
```

## Source freshness

Warehouse required.

```bash
dbt source freshness --select source:<name>
```

## Result validation

Not generic shell commands. Generate from model grain and business logic; put exact SQL in the output contract:

- Grain uniqueness query
- Null-key query
- Before/after row count
- Metric reconciliation
- Duplicate incremental-key query
- Second incremental-run comparison
- Dashboard field and exposure check
- Orchestration selectors if model names or tags changed (confirm in Civis; do not invent job names)

Runbook: see repo overlay (e.g. `docs/runbooks/schema-change-review.md`).
